  <p><strong>How much does it cost to manufacture leggings, sports bras and yoga sets?</strong> The honest answer is: it depends on the product specification and order plan. A basic listing image does not tell a supplier the fabric quality, construction complexity, color count, size ratio, branding, packaging or delivery basis needed to make a comparable quote.</p>

  <p>For B2B buyers, the goal is not to chase an unexplained low number. It is to understand which decisions move the cost, which details are included, and how to compare two activewear manufacturer quotes on the same commercial basis. This guide gives you a practical framework for leggings, sports bras and coordinated yoga sets.</p>

  <h2>Why the same activewear category can cost very differently</h2>
  <p>Two pairs of leggings can both look black and high-waisted, yet have different yarn composition, fabric weight, opacity target, panel count, pocket construction, waistband finish, printing, labels and packing. The same applies to sports bras: a simple pull-on style is not costed in the same way as a lined, adjustable or multi-panel support style.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Product category</th><th>Cost drivers that often change most</th><th>Questions to settle before quoting</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Leggings</td><td>Fabric consumption, opacity target, panels, pockets, waistband construction, seam placement and length.</td><td>Full length, 7/8 or capri? Pocket or no pocket? Fabric composition, colors, sizes and logo treatment?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sports bras</td><td>Support construction, lining, elastic, pads, straps, adjusters, hardware and grading.</td><td>Low, medium or high-support intent? Removable pads? Adjustable straps? Required size range?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Yoga sets</td><td>Combined components, matching color consistency, fabric use across top and bottom, coordinated packaging and size mix.</td><td>Which pieces are included? One fabric or mixed fabrics? Will top and bottom be packed and sold together?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The seven building blocks of an activewear manufacturing cost</h2>
  <h3>1. Fabric and material selection</h3>
  <p>Fabric is more than a percentage printed on a label. Composition, knit structure, weight, hand feel, stretch, recovery, color and finish affect how much material is used and how the garment is built. A buyer selecting yoga leggings for cool-weather coverage may make a different material choice from a buyer launching lightweight summer training sets. Our <a href="/blogs/fabric-weight-selection-guide/">fabric GSM guide</a> explains why fabric weight is a useful comparison point, but not the only performance measure.</p>

  <h3>2. Pattern, cutting and sewing complexity</h3>
  <p>Every added panel, curved seam, pocket, contrast insert, binding line, strap adjustment or hardware detail creates another operation to control. Construction choices should be judged by the value they add to the wearer and the brand—not only by how impressive they look in a reference image.</p>

  <h3>3. Quantity, colors and size mix</h3>
  <p>Manufacturing quantity is not just the total number of garments. A 500-piece order split across five colors and four sizes may operate differently from 500 pieces concentrated in one colorway. Send the expected units by style, color and size so the manufacturer can discuss a practical MOQ route. See the <a href="/guides/moq/">activewear MOQ guide</a> for a buyer-focused starting point.</p>

  <h3>4. Branding, labels and packaging</h3>
  <p>Private-label activewear cost planning should include logo execution, main labels, size labels, care labels, hang tags, barcodes, polybags and carton requirements. These are not afterthoughts: they affect sourcing, approval and packing. Decide early whether your goal is a clean ready-stock presentation or a fully branded retail launch.</p>

  <h3>5. Sampling and development</h3>
  <p>Development work may be needed to turn a concept into a repeatable garment. The more open decisions there are around fit, fabric, components or graphics, the more valuable a clear sample process becomes. Use our <a href="/blogs/activewear-sample-approval-checklist/">activewear sample approval checklist</a> to make feedback clearer before an order moves forward.</p>

  <h3>6. Commercial and delivery basis</h3>
  <p>A quote becomes meaningful only when the delivery basis is stated. Product cost, packing, inland handling, freight, duties and destination-side charges are different parts of an overall landed-cost discussion. Ask what is included, which currency is used, and what assumptions still need confirmation instead of comparing unlabeled totals.</p>

  <h3>7. Risk allowance and change control</h3>
  <p>Late changes to colors, fabrics, artwork, size splits or packaging can affect the original plan. Buyers reduce surprises by locking the key inputs before bulk production and documenting what must be approved before changes are made.</p>

  <h2>How to request like-for-like supplier quotes</h2>
  <p>Send the same brief to every supplier. That creates a far better comparison than asking each one to interpret a photo independently.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Style reference or tech pack, including front, back and detail views.</li>
    <li>Fabric composition, desired hand feel and performance priorities.</li>
    <li>Size range and estimated quantity by size and color.</li>
    <li>Logo, label, hang tag, barcode and packaging requirements.</li>
    <li>Target market, requested delivery basis and any launch date constraints.</li>
    <li>A request for the supplier to identify what is included and what remains to be confirmed.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Use cost as a design decision, not just a negotiation point</h2>
  <p>The best sourcing decision often balances unit cost, sell-through potential, product comfort, quality risk, branding need and speed to launch. A buyer might choose a smaller ready-stock program for an early market test, then develop a more customized yoga set after validating demand. Another buyer might prioritize a signature fabric and label presentation from the beginning. Both approaches can be valid when the specification and commercial plan are clear.</p>
